# Keep the kernel's generic ftdi_sio driver off an FT232H so openFPGALoader
# (which talks to it as a libusb MPSSE/JTAG device) can claim it.
#
# Without this the kernel binds it as a USB serial converter and hands it a
# ttyUSB node, then fights libusb for the device. Measured on 2026-08-31:
# ftdi_sio attached and detached six times over a few minutes, once per
# openFPGALoader invocation, and an SPI-flash write failed part-way through
# with a misread JEDEC ID and write-enable timeouts.
#
# This is the same class of problem as needing Zadig/WinUSB on Windows -- a
# general-purpose serial driver claiming a device that is not being used as a
# serial port.
#
# NOTE it is not a complete fix on its own: openFPGALoader resets the device
# when it opens MPSSE mode, and the kernel can win the re-probe race that
# follows. Scripts doing anything long-running (a flash write) should still
# unbind explicitly immediately beforehand:
#
#   echo -n '1-1.1:1.0' > /sys/bus/usb/drivers/ftdi_sio/unbind
#
# with the interface path from `ls /sys/bus/usb/drivers/ftdi_sio/`.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0403", ATTRS{idProduct}=="6014", DRIVER=="ftdi_sio", RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'echo $kernel > /sys/bus/usb/drivers/ftdi_sio/unbind'"
